+# Rhythm Heaven (US) — recompile and port plan
+
+Drafted 2026-10-09 from a read-only survey; decisions and suite added same day. Facts about the ROM, repo and
+machine are in the "TARGET: Rhythm Heaven" section of `CLAUDE.md`; this file
+is only the order of work. Phases are ordered by dependency. Each ends in an
+exit test that can pass or fail.
+
+Standing rules: PRINCIPLES.md / DEBUG.md. Validation is limited by
+`AGENTS.md` (one gameplay run per kind, no benchmark matrices).
+
+## Decisions (settled 2026-10-09)
+
+| # | Decision | Outcome |
+|---|---|---|
+| D1 | BIOS/firmware | No retail dumps on hand. Use the **FreeBIOS path** (`third_party/freebios`, BSD-2, hashes verified by the suite) plus generated firmware. Retail dumps stay the authority for any accuracy claim. Note: this is mstan's FreeBIOS, **not** melonDS's built-in replacement; an oracle diff only works if `ndsref` loads the same `drastic_bios_*.bin` and the same generated firmware (not yet verified, Phase 1). |
+| D2 | Title repo | `rhythmheavenrecomp`. Created at `~/dev/rhythmheavenrecomp` (outside iCloud Drive, which holds the framework checkout). |
+| D3 | iOS meaning | A user-built, self-signed app. No App Store, no prebuilt binary. See "Builder app". |
+| D4 | Windows verification | `tools/rh_suite.py` run by the user on Windows; results come back as `report.json`. |
+
+## Phase 0 — housekeeping (small, do first)
+
+1. Add `*.nds` and `melonDS.app/` to `.gitignore` (the 128 MiB ROM and the
+   app bundle are currently untracked but one `git add .` from being committed).
+2. Move working output out of iCloud: build trees, `generated/`, title banks.
+3. Create the title repo skeleton (D2) and pin this framework as a submodule.
+4. Install deps: `brew install sdl3 libslirp` (confirm what the runner CMake needs).
+
+Exit: clean `git status`; recompiler tests (including the three that take
+`<nds_recompile> <work-dir>` args) pass from the new build dir.
+
+## Phase 1 — runner builds and boots on macOS arm64
+
+Goal: the existing, proven firmware path runs on this Mac. No Rhythm Heaven yet.
+
+1. Make the runner configure on macOS: drop mingw-only link flags, remove the
+   `F:/Projects/recomp-ui` default, make `host_unwind_x64`/`host_profile`
+   compile out (or use a Mach-O unwinder) off Windows, build with
+   `-DNDS_SDL_BACKEND=SDL3`, `NDS_3D_RENDERER=soft`.
+2. Gate Tier-2 / live-overlay (`live_overlay*.cpp`) behind a CMake option,
+   default OFF on Apple targets. This is also the iOS prerequisite.
+3. Produce banks for the BIOSes (retail per D1, else FreeBIOS) and boot to the
+   firmware menu with mouse touch.
+4. Build the oracle (`oracle/setup-melonds.sh` → `ndsref`) on macOS and run G1.
+   Use `melonDS.app` (1.1) only for eyeballing, never as the diff reference.
+
+Exit: G1 passes on macOS with `tier3_insns9 = tier3_insns7 = 0`; menu is
+interactive with the mouse.
+
+## Phase 2 — cartridge boot of Rhythm Heaven (LLE)
+
+1. Boot the ROM through the real BIOS/firmware/card path (`startup_mode =
+   automatic`; secure area re-encrypt already exists). Watch
+   `dispatch_misses.log` and the cart ring to the first game frame.
+2. Determine the save type (melonDS DB or observed AUXSPI traffic) and set
+   `[cartridge]` in the title's `game.toml`. Add persistence if D3 in
+   PLAN.md (host `.sav`) is not already done.
+3. Run from Tier 3 first (the permitted dirty-RAM interpreter on the guest's
+   own bytes) purely to learn which regions are executed and what the
+   hardware touches. Tier 3 is a measuring instrument here, not the deliverable.
+
+Exit: boots to the Rhythm Heaven title screen on both CPUs with no
+unexplained unmapped I/O; save type recorded.
+
+## Phase 3 — static coverage (the core of "recompile")
+
+1. ARM9 main image: recompile from the ROM file at `0x02000000`; ARM7 at
+   `0x02380000`. Use exact entry points from the header plus function
+   discovery; fix any finder gap in the analyzer, not with config hints.
+2. Overlays: generate a bank per overlay (165) straight from the ROM's
+   overlay table, keyed by content with live-byte validation, because 105 + 59
+   + 1 bodies share three RAM addresses. If the framework lacks a
+   per-overlay-id/content-keyed emission mode (`--validate-live-bytes` is the
+   nearest existing flag), add it to the recompiler. Write a tool in the
+   title repo that reads the overlay table and emits the configs.
+3. Reconcile with a runtime capture: every PC the interpreter executed in
+   Phase 2 must be covered by a bank or be explained.
+4. Promote until Tier 3 is idle across a complete play session.
+
+Exit: `dispatch_misses.log` empty; `tier3_insns9`/`tier3_insns7` ≈ 0 over
+boot → title → menu → one minigame; `clean_ram_rejects = 0`.
+
+## Phase 4 — accuracy vs the oracle
+
+1. Byte-lock boot → title → a chosen minigame against fresh `ndsref`
+   (the G3 pattern: sync on hardware events, compare both framebuffers and
+   audio ordinals). First divergence only.
+2. Expect to meet the known gaps in PLAN.md WS-D as the game exercises them
+   (2D windows, display FIFO, SPU capture). Implement each only when the
+   diff demonstrates it, mirroring melonDS as the reference.
+3. Audio is part of correctness for a rhythm game: compare `audio_samples`
+   sample-exact on the same scenario; measure input → audio/video latency in
+   the interactive build.
+
+Exit: the chosen scenario is byte-locked including both framebuffers and audio;
+touch input drives the minigame.
+
+## Phase 5 — desktop deliverables
+
+- **macOS**: arm64 `.app` bundle (universal later if wanted), ROM and BIOS
+  picked by the user at first launch, saves under Application Support, a local
+  "regenerate banks from my ROM" script (needs Xcode CLT).
+- **Windows**: the project's primary supported toolchain (mingw64). Build in
+  CI, port the `.ps1` helper scripts' logic to portable Python where this
+  work depends on them, one user-run gameplay validation.
+- Shared: `game.toml` for the title (`[game].sha1`, `[cartridge]`,
+  `[display]`), key/gamepad bindings, stylus via mouse/trackpad.
+
+Exit: one documented command sequence per OS from "I have my ROM" to a
+playing game; G1 green on both.
+
+## Phase 6 — iOS
+
+Hard constraints from CLAUDE.md: no runtime codegen, GPL-3 source obligations,
+no ROM-derived banks in a shipped binary (D3).
+
+1. Build the runner + banks as static libraries under an Xcode project with
+   SDL3's iOS backend; Tier 2 off, soft renderer.
+2. Touch: the bottom screen is a native pointer surface; keep it a separate
+   path from the button abstraction (as WS-F F2 specifies).
+3. App shell: ROM/BIOS import through the Files picker into the sandbox,
+   saves in app storage, lifecycle (pause on background, flush the save and
+   firmware state on suspend), audio session and low-latency buffer config.
+4. Footprint: title banks are small compared to SM64DS, but confirm compile
+   time and binary size on device-class memory before committing to the layout.
+
+Exit: runs on a physical iPhone/iPad, stylus-equivalent touch works, the
+single gameplay validation run passes.
+
+## Builder app (the deliverable on every platform)
+
+Nothing playable can be shipped prebuilt (banks are ROM-derived), so the
+product is a small app that turns *your* ROM into *your* build:
+
+```
+ROM (+ optional BIOS/firmware dumps)
+   -> verify SHA-1, parse header/overlay table
+   -> nds_recompile (ARM9, ARM7, 165 overlays, content-keyed)
+   -> compile banks + runner with a bundled/located C toolchain
+   -> package: .exe (Windows) | .app (macOS) | .ipa (iOS, unsigned)
+```
+
+Constraints that shape it:
+
+- Windows `.exe`: needs a C/C++ toolchain on the machine. Either bundle one
+  (mingw-w64 / zig cc) or detect and guide. Decide after the Windows suite run.
+- macOS `.app`: needs Xcode command-line tools (or a bundled clang).
+- iOS `.ipa`: can only be built on a Mac with Xcode and Apple's iOS SDK. The
+  builder produces an unsigned `.ipa` / Xcode project; signing and install are
+  the user's (their Apple ID, AltStore/Sideloadly/Xcode). A Windows machine
+  cannot produce it.
+- It reuses the Phase 3 tooling; keep the pipeline a CLI first, then put a thin
+  GUI on it. The builder must never write ROM-derived output anywhere that is
+  uploaded or committed.
+
+Order: CLI pipeline (after Phase 3) -> macOS `.app` output (Phase 5) -> Windows
+`.exe` output -> iOS `.ipa` output (Phase 6) -> GUI shell.
+
+## Test suite (`tools/rh_suite.py`)
+
+Stdlib-only Python, Windows/macOS/Linux, no gameplay and no benchmarking
+(AGENTS.md). Stages: `env`, `rom`, `bios`, `recompiler`, `rom-recompile`,
+`runner` (opt-in, `--runner`). Writes `build-suite/report.json`.
+
+```
+python tools/rh_suite.py --rom "<path to .nds>" [--work <dir>] [--only env,rom] [--cc <compiler>]
+```
+
+First run on macOS arm64 (Apple clang 21), 2026-10-09: **34 pass, 0 fail,
+4 warn, 0 skip** with the ROM present. Confirms: the 6 recompiler tests; ROM
+identity and layout; ARM9 static image uncompressed with ITCM (0x5C0 B) and
+DTCM (0x60 B) autoload blocks; all 165 overlays uncompressed with in-bounds
+FAT entries; ARM9 (633 fns), ARM7 (1414 fns) and all 165 overlays (15,728 fns
+combined) decode with zero codegen gaps; emitted ARM9/ARM7 C compiles. Not yet
+run on Windows, and the runner stage has never passed anywhere in this work.
+
+What the suite does **not** prove: coverage. Entry-only discovery misses
+indirect-call targets (480 indirect sites in ARM9). Runtime-guided discovery
+is Phase 3.
+
+## Dependency graph
+
+```
+P0 → P1 → P2 → P3 → P4 → P5 (macOS, Windows) ─┐
+                      └──────────────→ P6 (iOS) ┘
+```
+
+P5 and P6 only need P1's portability work plus P4's correctness; they can
+overlap. P6 starts after P5-macOS because it reuses its app shell and config.
+
+## Biggest risks
+
+1. **Overlay handling**: 165 bodies at 3 addresses. If keyed by PC the game
+   will silently run the wrong function. Mitigation: content keys, fail-closed
+   guards, empty `dispatch_misses.log` as a hard gate.
+2. **No BIOS/firmware dumps** (D1). The accuracy claim depends on them.
+3. **Unimplemented 2D features** for a sprite-heavy game; unknown until the
+   first oracle diff.
+4. **Latency** on Apple platforms (audio buffers, vsync) in a timing-judged game.
+5. **iOS distribution**: licensing and ROM-derived code limit this to
+   self-built installs.
